---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy | Philosophy Defined"
  description="What Philosophy Defined collects, what it does not, and how visitor analytics are handled."
  canonical="/privacy"
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p>
        Short version: this archive stores no personal data, requires no
        account, and does not sell visitor data. Some pages may display
        advertising served by Google AdSense.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-layout">
      <aside class="prose-aside">
        <p>Plain language, because a privacy page written in legalese protects no one.</p>
      </aside>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>What is collected</h2>
        <p>
          Page visits are measured with Vercel Analytics and Ahrefs
          Analytics, which record pageviews and referrers without cookies
          and without building per-visitor profiles. No identifiers are
          attached to a visit; the archive cannot single out who read a
          passage, only how often pages were read.
        </p>

        <h2>Advertising</h2>
        <p>
          Google AdSense may show ads on some pages. AdSense can use cookies
          or similar technologies to serve and measure ads; that processing
          is governed by Google, not by this archive. Visitors can review
          Google's advertising settings at
          <a href="https://adssettings.google.com/">adssettings.google.com</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>What is not collected</h2>
        <p>
          No accounts, no email lists, and no sale of visitor data by this
          archive. The reading preferences (text size and colour scheme) are
          stored in your browser's local storage only; they never leave your
          device. Advertising identifiers, if any, are handled by Google
          AdSense as described above.
        </p>

        <h2>Sharing tools</h2>
        <p>
          The share button constructs a link in your browser and hands it to
          the platform you choose. The archive's server is not involved in a
          share; what a platform does with a shared link is governed by that
          platform.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about this page can go to the address on the{" "}
          <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
